      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.104,
            0.127,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "30"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.783,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "MATHEMATICS"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.139,
            0.754,
            0.159
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3.3 Algebraic Methods of Solving a Pair of Linear Equations"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.174,
            0.901,
            0.341
          ],
          "content": "**In the previous section, we discussed how to solve a pair of linear equations graphically. The graphical method is not convenient in cases when the point representing the solution of the linear equations has non-integral coordinates like** $\\left( { \\sqrt { 3 } }   ,   2 { \\sqrt { 7 } } \\right)$ $(-1.75,3.3),\\left(\\frac{4}{13},\\frac{1}{19}\\right)$ , etc. There is every possibility of making mistakes while reading such coordinates. Is there any alternative method of finding the solution? There are several algebraic methods, which we shall now discuss."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.353,
            0.902,
            0.393
          ],
          "content": "**3.3.1 Substitution Method : We shall explain the method of substitution by taking some examples.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.406,
            0.802,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": "**Example 4 : Solve the following pair of equations by substitution method:**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.381,
            0.431,
            0.898,
            0.451
          ],
          "content": "7 x - 1 5 y = 2\\tag{1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.401,
            0.456,
            0.897,
            0.476
          ],
          "content": "x + 2y = 3\\tag{2}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.482,
            0.205,
            0.5
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.507,
            0.899,
            0.545
          ],
          "content": "**Step 1 : We pick either of the equations and write one variable in terms of the other. Let us consider the Equation (2) :**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.582,
            0.242,
            0.6
          ],
          "content": "**and write it as**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.401,
            0.556,
            0.898,
            0.601
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}x + 2y &= 3 \\\\x &= 3 \\geq 2y\\end{aligned}\\tag{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.607,
            0.645,
            0.626
          ],
          "content": "**Step 2 : Substitute the value of** x **in Equation (1). We get**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.658,
            0.139,
            0.676
          ],
          "content": "**i.e.,**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.684,
            0.139,
            0.702
          ],
          "content": "**i.e.,**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.318,
            0.632,
            0.537,
            0.702
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{aligned}7(3 - 2y) - 15y &= 2 \\\\21 - 14y - 15y &= 2 \\\\\\angle \\quad - 29y &= - 19\\end{aligned}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.723,
            0.205,
            0.742
          ],
          "content": "**Therefore,**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.45,
            0.711,
            0.533,
            0.75
          ],
          "content": "y = { \\frac { 1 9 } { 2 9 } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.755,
            0.659,
            0.775
          ],
          "content": "**Step 3 : Substituting this value of** y **in Equation (3), we get**"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.449,
            0.784,
            0.667,
            0.828
          ],
          "content": "x = 3 - 2\\left(\\frac{19}{29}\\right) = \\frac{49}{29}",
          "image_source": "images/page_6_equation_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.827,
            0.51,
            0.868
          ],
          "content": "**Therefore, the solution is** $x = \\frac { 4 9 } { 2 9 }   ,   y = \\frac { 1 9 } { 2 9 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.967,
            0.563,
            0.982
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
